Add a ref to track the active camera request ID to prevent concurrent `getUserMedia` calls from leaking camera streams.

```suggestion
  const streamRef = useRef<MediaStream | null>(null)
  const ocrClientRef = useRef<OcrClient | null>(null)
  const matcherRef = useRef<BrandMatcher | null>(null)
  const voterRef = useRef(new TemporalVoter())
  const detectionActiveRef = useRef(true)
  const activeRequestRef = useRef<number>(0)
```

![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

Since `startCamera` is asynchronous, rapid toggles of `facingMode` or visibility changes can trigger multiple concurrent `getUserMedia` requests. This can lead to multiple active camera streams, causing hardware resource leaks (the camera light staying on) and potential failures on subsequent requests. We should use a request counter to ensure only the latest stream is kept and any obsolete streams are stopped immediately.

```
  const startCamera = useCallback(async () => {
    const requestId = ++activeRequestRef.current
    stopStream()
    setCameraStatus("starting")
    try {
      const stream = await navigator.mediaDevices.getUserMedia({
        video: {
          facingMode,
          width: { ideal: 1280 },
          height: { ideal: 720 },
        },
      })
      if (requestId !== activeRequestRef.current) {
        for (const track of stream.getTracks()) {
          track.stop()
        }
        return
      }
      streamRef.current = stream
      if (videoRef.current) {
        videoRef.current.srcObject = stream
      }
      setCameraStatus("active")
    } catch (error) {
      if (requestId === activeRequestRef.current) {
        console.error("Error accessing the camera:", error)
        setCameraStatus(statusFromCameraError(error))
      }
    }
  }, [facingMode, stopStream])
```

![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The `onerror` handler is currently defined inside the `readyPromise` executor. Once `readyPromise` resolves, any subsequent runtime errors or crashes in the Web Worker (e.g., WASM out-of-memory) will not be handled, and any pending requests in `this.pending` will hang indefinitely. We should define a global `onerror` handler on the worker that rejects all pending requests and transitions the client to an error state.

```typescript
  constructor() {
    this.worker = new Worker(new URL("./ocr-worker.ts", import.meta.url))
    let rejectReady: ((error: Error) => void) | null = null
    this.readyPromise = new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      rejectReady = reject
      this.worker.onmessage = (event: MessageEvent<WorkerResponse>) => {
        const message = event.data
        if (message.type === "ready") {
          this.ready = true
          resolve()
          return
        }
        if (message.type === "init-error") {
          reject(new Error(message.message))
          return
        }
        this.settle(message)
      }
    })
    this.worker.onerror = (event) => {
      const error = new Error(event.message || "OCR worker crashed")
      if (!this.ready && rejectReady) {
        rejectReady(error)
      }
      for (const request of this.pending.values()) {
        request.reject(error)
      }
      this.pending.clear()
    }
  }
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

In `stopStream`, we stop the tracks of the stream, but we don't clear `videoRef.current.srcObject`. This can leave the video element in a frozen state or holding onto the stream reference. It is best practice to set `videoRef.current.srcObject = null` to release the resource completely.

```
  const stopStream = useCallback(() => {
    for (const track of streamRef.current?.getTracks() ?? []) {
      track.stop()
    }
    streamRef.current = null
    if (videoRef.current) {
      videoRef.current.srcObject = null
    }
  }, [])
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

The current percentile calculation has a logic error. If `cumulative` is 0 for several bins, `low` is updated to the last bin with 0 cumulative count, which is incorrect (the 2nd percentile should be the first bin where cumulative count reaches the target). Similarly, `high` is updated to a value where cumulative count is strictly less than `highTarget`, which can result in a value that has no pixels at all. A more robust approach is to find the first bin where the cumulative sum is greater than or equal to the target.

```suggestion
  let cumulative = 0
  let low = 0
  let high = MAX_LUMA
  let lowFound = false
  let highFound = false
  for (let value = 0; value <= MAX_LUMA; value++) {
    cumulative += histogram[value]
    if (!lowFound && cumulative >= lowTarget) {
      low = value
      lowFound = true
    }
    if (!highFound && cumulative >= highTarget) {
      high = value
      highFound = true
    }
  }
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

If `detect` is called after `dispose()` has been called, it will attempt to post a message to a terminated worker. This request will be added to `this.pending` and will hang indefinitely. We should reject the promise immediately if the client has been disposed.

```typescript
  detect(imageData: ImageData): Promise<DetectResult> {
    if (this.disposed) {
      return Promise.reject(new Error("OCR client is disposed"))
    }
    const id = this.nextId++
    const request: DetectRequest = {
      id,
      type: "detect",
      width: imageData.width,
      height: imageData.height,
      pixels: imageData.data.buffer,
    }
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      this.pending.set(id, { resolve, reject })
      this.worker.postMessage(request, [request.pixels])
    })
  }
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

Set the `disposed` flag to true when disposing the client.

```typescript
  dispose(): void {
    this.disposed = true
    this.worker.terminate()
    for (const request of this.pending.values()) {
      request.reject(new Error("OCR client disposed"))
    }
    this.pending.clear()
  }
```

